        Microstructure of Concentrated Liquidity: Ticks, Bands, and Discrete Price Curves
      </h2>
      <p>
        In traditional constant product Automated Market Makers (such as Uniswap v2), liquidity is distributed uniformly along the hyperbola $x \cdot y = k$ across all possible price points from zero to infinity. While mathematically simple, this structural design is extraordinarily inefficient: in stablecoin or high-volume blue-chip pools, over 99% of deposited capital sits completely idle at prices far removed from current trading levels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Uniswap v3 introduces <strong>concentrated liquidity</strong>, allowing liquidity providers (LPs) to allocate capital exclusively within a bounded price interval $[P_a, P_b]$. Within that designated interval, the pool behaves identically to a virtual constant product curve with substantially amplified reserves.
      </p>
      <p>
        To make continuous mathematical curves computable inside Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) smart contracts with discrete integer math, Uniswap v3 discretizes the price domain into integer-indexed <strong>ticks</strong>. The price at any discrete tick $i$ is defined by:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-amber-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>Price(i) = 1.0001^i
</code></pre>

      <p>
        Because each tick represents a 1 basis point (0.01% or 0.0001) change in price, tracking every individual tick in high-volatility pairs would consume excessive gas during swaps. Therefore, Uniswap v3 couples each fee tier with a specific <strong>tick spacing parameter</strong> (<code>t_s</code>), restricting active liquidity boundaries to ticks where <code>i % t_s == 0</code>.
      </p>

